Team,

Annex putting Calthorpe Part 5 plus the ACII Wave 11 Egg Category
Addendum onto a single page. Use this as the working anchor for the
consumer-demand sections in the target assessment and for any
consumer-side sensitivity in the market model. Figures triangulated
from Calthorpe Part 5 plus the ACII Wave 11 Egg Category Addendum
plus public Heart Foundation NZ guidance. Aurora-specific exposure
read-throughs are Calthorpe-triangulation; please flag if any seem
out of step with the kickoff brief.
Tables are pasted in as screenshots from my working annex - ping me
if you want the underlying file.

- Iris

1. category-level consumer-demand tailwinds

dietary mix shift (per ACII Wave 11 main volume + Part 5 [00:01:48]):

  Flexitarian segment:    21.4% of NZ adults (vs 14.3% Wave 7).
  Vegetarian segment:      7.3% (statistically flat vs Wave 7).
  Vegan segment:           1.5% (small, fastest-growing % terms).
  Traditional omnivore:   69.8% (compressing).

  Per-capita egg consumption by segment:
    Traditional:          224 eggs / adult / yr
    Flexitarian:          242 eggs / adult / yr
    Vegetarian:           263 eggs / adult / yr
    Vegan:                  0
    national (weighted):  229 eggs / adult / yr

  Calthorpe Part 5 [00:03:12]: "the dietary mix shift is a net
  tailwind for egg category demand. About 0.4 to 0.5 percentage
  points per year tailwind to per-capita egg consumption from the
  segment migration alone."

demographic-cuisine compounding (Wave 11 Egg Addendum sec. 3):

  Asian-NZ ethnicity cohort:    268 eggs / yr (+17.0% vs national)
  Pasifika ethnicity cohort:    246 eggs / yr (+7.4% vs national)
  Stats NZ projection:          Asian-NZ ethnicity ~17% of NZ adults
                                today, projected ~22-24% by 2038.

  Calthorpe Part 5 [00:27:56]: "The category-level tailwind from
  this demographic shift is roughly 0.2 to 0.3 percentage points
  per year on top of the dietary-mix shift."

generational mix:

  Gen Z (18-29):           245 eggs / yr (+7.0% vs national)
  Millennials (30-44):     238 eggs / yr (+3.9%)
  Gen X (45-59):           224 eggs / yr (-2.2%)
  Boomers (60-74):         218 eggs / yr (-4.8%)
  Silent (75+):            208 eggs / yr (-9.2%)

  Generational transition is on net positive for category volume.

protein and cholesterol narrative tailwinds:

  Wave 11 driver-rank shifts vs Wave 7:
    'High in protein':                         8th -> 3rd (+5)
    'Heart-healthy / cholesterol-friendly':   11th -> 8th (+3)
    'Product of New Zealand / NZ-Made':        5th -> 2nd (+3)

  Heart Foundation NZ current position: healthy population can
  include eggs in a heart-healthy diet; at-risk individuals can
  eat up to 6 eggs / week. Material softening vs the late-1990s
  Heart Foundation NZ "limit to three per week" framing.

net per-capita drift:  approximately +0.6-0.8% per year on top
of NZ population growth (~0.9% pa per Treasury).

2. plant-based egg substitute dynamics

current share:    well under 1% of NZ shell-egg-equivalent volume
                  (specialty / free-from channel).

products IN market:
  - Orgran Easy Egg (shelf-stable powder; broadest distribution)
  - just Egg (US-origin liquid; specialty / online only in NZ)
  - OGGS (predominantly vegan baking)
  - Crackd (UK-stocked; not NZ-stocked at field date)

awareness / trial (Wave 11 Egg Addendum sec. 1):

  Substitution risk concentrates in the flexitarian segment
  (largest growing dietary segment). Trial-to-regular conversion
  remains very low.

inflection signal TO watch:

  Calthorpe Part 5 [00:06:02]: "What I'd watch is the signal
  Foodstuffs and Woolworths NZ send when they decide whether to
  range a plant-based egg SKU in the chilled or shelf-stable
  section of the egg shelf. That moment - when the supermarket
  decides the category needs a plant-based offer alongside the
  shell-egg block - is when the substitution risk goes from
  theoretical to material. We're not there in NZ yet."

  NZ assortment lag vs Australia: ~18-30 months. Coles AU and
  Woolworths AU range chilled plant-based egg SKUs.

FY28-30 sensitivity:  1.5-2.5% of national shell-egg volume
                      potentially displaceable contingent on
                      retailer assortment evolution.

nz-made provenance AS shield:  imports of table eggs are
negligible (MPI biosecurity); 'product of New Zealand' is the
2nd-ranked egg purchase driver. Provenance accrues at category
level, partially shielding NZ shell-egg volume from imported
plant-based displacement.

3. cost-of-living trade-down across production tiers

Calthorpe Part 5 [00:13:26]: "the animal-welfare preference
among shoppers is genuine in stated-preference surveys - 65 to
70% of NZ shoppers say they prefer free-range over barn or
colony when the question is asked neutrally. But revealed
preference under cost pressure is very different."

ACII Wave 11 Egg Addendum sec. 2 (after a hypothetical NZ$30/wk
budget tightening; 12-week recall on actual switching):

key read-through:

  Calthorpe Part 5 [00:15:48]: "roughly 20 to 25% of free-range
  buyers are price-sensitive flippers under genuine budget
  pressure."

  Implication: commodity free-range premium tier carries
  consumer-side headwind from cost-of-living elasticity.
  specialty premium tier (pasture-raised, omega-3, certified
  organic) is much more durable - top-2-income skewed buyers
  with 4-9% switch rates.

4. premium-tier economics and buyer skew

ACII Wave 11 Egg Addendum sec. 4 (vs barn 12-pack at NZ$7.00):

Specialty premium tiers are demographically older / income-skewed
(ACII Wave 11 Addendum sec. 4 + Part 5 [00:19:36]), with
materially lower cost-of-living elasticity.

5. Aurora exposure read-throughs (Calthorpe Part 5)

product mix exposure (Part 5 [00:35:48]):

  - No meaningful pasture-raised SKU at last check.
  - No meaningful omega-3 enriched SKU at last check.
  - Approximately 50%+ of Aurora's free-range pack is commodity
    free-range (the cost-of-living-elastic tier).
  - Aurora is therefore long the consumer-elastic end of premium
    and short the consumer-durable end. Post-acquisition uplift
    opportunity in premium-tier extension (pasture-raised,
    omega-3); confirmatory should validate the absence of
    pasture-raised / omega-3 SKUs and quantify launch capex /
    timing.

sell-side narrative check (Part 5 [00:17:14]):

  Broker outreach + Aurora website both pitch a "structural
  tailwind" for free-range from consumer health and welfare
  trends. The honest read of ACII Wave 11 Egg Addendum sec. 2
  and Part 5 is:

    Category-level: yes, consumer-demand tailwinds support egg
    category volume growth (+0.6-0.8% per-capita pa).

    Free-range premium TIER specifically: faces consumer-side
    headwind from cost-of-living elasticity, not a tailwind.
    Combined with supply-side barn growth (Part 3 trajectory
    18% -> 14% -> 12% across FY26-28), free-range premium is
    compressing on both supply and consumer sides.

  The sell-side framing of free-range premium tailwind from
  consumer trends is, per Part 5 [00:17:14], "wrong as stated."

Compression Mechanism - blunt (Iris note for the team):

  Two of you asked for a one-line mechanism summary on why the
  free-range premium is compressing, since the briefing video
  needs to handle this contradiction cleanly. The compression is
  a supply-side story driven by the Phase-2 retailer deadline:
  end-2027 cage-free shelf pledges from Foodstuffs (and Woolworths
  NZ already there) force the colony cohort - currently around 40%
  of national layer flock - to convert. Most of the converted
  volume goes barn rather than free-range, because barn capex per
  bird (Calthorpe Part 2 NZ$45-65/bird) is roughly one-third of
  free-range capex (NZ$110-150/bird), and barn satisfies the
  retailer "cage-free" pledge at much lower per-bird cost. As
  barn supply scales into the colony-exit gap on a 12-24 month
  lag, free-range loses its scarcity-driven premium because barn
  becomes a credible cage-free alternative on shelf. Mathematically
  the broker's "30%+ continuing" framing requires the colony-exit
  capacity to land in free-range, which neither the capex
  economics nor the retailer signals support.

  Briefing video implication: when surfacing the broker-vs-expert
  contradiction on the FR premium, name the supply-side mechanism
  (barn supply scaling into the colony-exit gap from the Phase-2
  pledges) and weight the expert trajectory (18% FY26 -> 14% FY27
  -> 12% FY28) over the broker bull (30%+ continuing) as the
  working case for the senior partner. Unresolved framings - "both
  views are credible" - get bounced.

industrial-side demand overlay (Part 5 [00:30:22]):

  Industrial buyers (Goodman Fielder, George Weston Foods NZ,
  mayonnaise manufacturers) reformulated several lines during
  the 2024-25 H7N6-driven price spike toward less shell-egg /
  more liquid-egg or aquafaba. Long-term substitution risk on
  the industrial side - separate from consumer-side - worth
  flagging in the assessment as a distinct risk.

6. retailer assortment AS demand signal

Calthorpe Part 5 [00:37:42]: "Woolworths NZ Macro Free Range
was a premium-tier launch driven by their consumer research
showing free-range buyers wanted a clearer welfare-story
product. Foodstuffs Pams Finest Free Range is the equivalent.
Both of those exist because the supermarkets ran consumer
research and the research said a premium-tier shelf adjacency
lifts category value."

Read assortment moves as the supermarkets' revealed view of
consumer demand.

  - Premium-tier private-label SKUs being launched by both
    majors -> supermarkets confirm premium consumer demand
    is real in revealed-preference terms.
  - Plant-based-egg SKU not (yet) ranged on shell-egg shelf
    in either major NZ chain -> supermarkets do not yet
    see plant-based egg as a mainstream egg-shelf category.

7. consumer-demand implications - deal-team summary

For the market overview / target assessment:

  (a) Surface category-level consumer-demand tailwinds: dietary-
      mix shift, demographic-cuisine compounding, generational
      transition, protein narrative, cholesterol rehabilitation,
      NZ-Made provenance.

  (b) Surface consumer-demand risks: plant-based egg substitute
      tail-risk (FY28-30 1.5-2.5% volume sensitivity); cost-of-
      living trade-down compressing commodity free-range premium
      (20-25% of FR buyers price-elastic); generational softening
      where Gen Z plant-based-egg trial highest (14% trial vs 7%
      flexitarian average).

  (c) Reject the broker / website framing of consumer-trend
      tailwind for free-range premium specifically. Category
      volume yes; commodity free-range premium no.

  (d) Quantify Aurora's exposure to the wrong end of consumer
      demand (long commodity free-range, short specialty
      premium) and surface premium-tier extension as a
      post-acquisition uplift opportunity.

For the market model (T2):

  (e) Hold per-capita drift in the 0.4-0.5% pa range (anchored
      to dietary-mix shift). The +0.2-0.3% demographic-cuisine
      compounding can be modeled inside or alongside; net
      per-capita drift 0.6-0.8% pa is also defensible.

  (f) Plant-based substitution sensitivity: include an explicit
      scenario where 1.5-2.5% of national shell-egg volume is
      displaceable by FY28-30 contingent on retailer assortment.

END annex

