<p>Broker outreach + Aurora website both pitch a &quot;structural tailwind&quot; for free-range from consumer health and welfare trends. The honest read of ACII Wave 11 Egg Addendum sec. 2 and Part 5 is:</p>
<p>Category-level: yes, consumer-demand tailwinds support egg category volume growth (+0.6-0.8% per-capita pa).</p>
<p>Free-range premium TIER specifically: faces consumer-side headwind from cost-of-living elasticity, not a tailwind. Combined with supply-side barn growth (Part 3 trajectory 18% -&gt; 14% -&gt; 12% across FY26-28), free-range premium is compressing on both supply and consumer sides.</p>
<p>The sell-side framing of free-range premium tailwind from consumer trends is, per Part 5 [00:17:14], &quot;wrong as stated.&quot;</p>
<p>Compression Mechanism - blunt (Iris note for the team):</p>
<p>Two of you asked for a one-line mechanism summary on why the free-range premium is compressing, since the briefing video needs to handle this contradiction cleanly. The compression is a supply-side story driven by the Phase-2 retailer deadline: end-2027 cage-free shelf pledges from Foodstuffs (and Woolworths NZ already there) force the colony cohort - currently around 40% of national layer flock - to convert. Most of the converted volume goes barn rather than free-range, because barn capex per bird (Calthorpe Part 2 NZ$45-65/bird) is roughly one-third of free-range capex (NZ$110-150/bird), and barn satisfies the retailer &quot;cage-free&quot; pledge at much lower per-bird cost. As barn supply scales into the colony-exit gap on a 12-24 month lag, free-range loses its scarcity-driven premium because barn becomes a credible cage-free alternative on shelf. Mathematically the broker&#x27;s &quot;30%+ continuing&quot; framing requires the colony-exit capacity to land in free-range, which neither the capex economics nor the retailer signals support.</p>
<p>Briefing video implication: when surfacing the broker-vs-expert contradiction on the FR premium, name the supply-side mechanism (barn supply scaling into the colony-exit gap from the Phase-2 pledges) and weight the expert trajectory (18% FY26 -&gt; 14% FY27 -&gt; 12% FY28) over the broker bull (30%+ continuing) as the working case for the senior partner. Unresolved framings - &quot;both views are credible&quot; - get bounced.</p>
<p>industrial-side demand overlay (Part 5 [00:30:22]):</p>
<p>Industrial buyers (Goodman Fielder, George Weston Foods NZ, mayonnaise manufacturers) reformulated several lines during the 2024-25 H7N6-driven price spike toward less shell-egg / more liquid-egg or aquafaba. Long-term substitution risk on the industrial side - separate from consumer-side - worth flagging in the assessment as a distinct risk.</p>
<h3>6. retailer assortment AS demand signal</h3>
<p>Calthorpe Part 5 [00:37:42]: &quot;Woolworths NZ Macro Free Range was a premium-tier launch driven by their consumer research showing free-range buyers wanted a clearer welfare-story product. Foodstuffs Pams Finest Free Range is the equivalent. Both of those exist because the supermarkets ran consumer research and the research said a premium-tier shelf adjacency lifts category value.&quot;</p>
<p>Read assortment moves as the supermarkets&#x27; revealed view of consumer demand.</p>
<p>- Premium-tier private-label SKUs being launched by both majors -&gt; supermarkets confirm premium consumer demand is real in revealed-preference terms. - Plant-based-egg SKU not (yet) ranged on shell-egg shelf in either major NZ chain -&gt; supermarkets do not yet see plant-based egg as a mainstream egg-shelf category.</p>
<h3>7. consumer-demand implications - deal-team summary</h3>
<p>For the market overview / target assessment:</p>
<p>(a) Surface category-level consumer-demand tailwinds: dietary- mix shift, demographic-cuisine compounding, generational transition, protein narrative, cholesterol rehabilitation, NZ-Made provenance.</p>
<p>(b) Surface consumer-demand risks: plant-based egg substitute tail-risk (FY28-30 1.5-2.5% volume sensitivity); cost-of- living trade-down compressing commodity free-range premium (20-25% of FR buyers price-elastic); generational softening where Gen Z plant-based-egg trial highest (14% trial vs 7% flexitarian average).</p>
<p>(c) Reject the broker / website framing of consumer-trend tailwind for free-range premium specifically. Category volume yes; commodity free-range premium no.</p>
<p>(d) Quantify Aurora&#x27;s exposure to the wrong end of consumer demand (long commodity free-range, short specialty premium) and surface premium-tier extension as a post-acquisition uplift opportunity.</p>
<p>For the market model (T2):</p>
